<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kharavela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kharavela was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, of the Mahameghavahana dynasty, who probably reigned in the 1st century BCE. He is known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a> at Udayagiri, which records his public works, festivals and campaigns against the Satavahanas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and kingdoms of the south. A devout Jain, he and his queen sponsored the rock-cut monastic caves of Udayagiri and Khandagiri.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Madhyamika, and the Yuga Purana prophesies that they would reach Pataliputra and cast down its mud walls before civil war called them home; whether such a campaign took place is disputed, and the Hathigumpha inscription has King <b>Kharavela</b> of Kalinga driving a demoralised Yavana army back to Mathura.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Satakarni is also named as a western rival in the Hathigumpha inscription of <b>Kharavela</b> of Kalinga, and an inscription on the southern gateway of the Great Stupa at Sanchi records a gift by a foreman of the artisans of King Siri Satakarni.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east, King <b>Kharavela</b> of Kalinga, in the 2nd or 1st century BCE, recorded his devotion to the Jinas in the Hathigumpha inscription near Bhubaneswar.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tolkāppiyam is the earliest text to use it, and around 150 BCE the Hathigumpha inscription of <b>Kharavela</b>, the Jain king of Kalinga, names a Tamira Samghatta, a Tamil confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashoka’s inscriptions refer to independent states beyond his southern frontier, and <b>Kharavela</b>’s Hathigumpha inscription records the destruction of a “confederacy of Tamil powers”; the region mostly remained unconquered by the Maurya and Gupta empires while having exchanges with them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The inscription records year by year the deeds of <b>Kharavela</b>, a devout Jain: he repaired the capital Kalinganagari after a cyclone, dug canals, staged festivals and led campaigns against the Satavahana dynasty to the west, against Magadha, from which he recovered a Jain image carried off by a…</blockquote><a href="/state/mahameghavahana-dynasty">Mahameghavahana dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
